          "en": "A program waiting for connections is said to be **listening** on a port. But it also chooses **on\nwhich address** it listens, and that is where everything is decided:\n\n| Listening address | Who can connect? |\n|---|---|\n| `127.0.0.1` (or `::1` in IPv6) | Only programs on the machine itself |\n| `192.168.1.20` (one specific address) | Machines that can reach that address |\n| `0.0.0.0` (or `::` in IPv6) | **Everyone**, through every network card |\n\nSeen from outside, a port can be in three states:\n\n| State | What happens | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| Open | The server answers `SYN-ACK` | A program is listening |\n| Closed | The server answers `RST` (refusal) | No program is listening |\n| Filtered | No answer at all | A firewall drops the packets |\n\nEvery open port is a possible way in: the **attack surface** of a machine is first measured by the\nnumber of ports it exposes.\n"
