        "code": "# dig = the reference tool to query DNS (package \"dnsutils\" on Debian/Ubuntu, \"bind-utils\" on RHEL)\n# Syntax: dig @resolver name TYPE\ndig @1.1.1.1 example.com A\n",
        "output": "; <<>> DiG 9.18.30 <<>> @1.1.1.1 example.com A\n; (1 server found)\n;; global options: +cmd\n;; Got answer:\n;; ->>HEADER<<- opcode: QUERY, status: NOERROR, id: 41712\n;; flags: qr rd ra ad; QUERY: 1, ANSWER: 2, AUTHORITY: 0, ADDITIONAL: 1\n\n;; OPT PSEUDOSECTION:\n; EDNS: version: 0, flags:; udp: 1232\n;; QUESTION SECTION:\n;example.com.                   IN      A\n\n;; ANSWER SECTION:\nexample.com.            205     IN      A       172.66.147.243\nexample.com.            205     IN      A       104.20.23.154\n\n;; Query time: 12 msec\n;; SERVER: 1.1.1.1#53(1.1.1.1) (UDP)\n;; WHEN: Fri Nov 27 14:29:30 CET 2026\n;; MSG SIZE  rcvd: 72\n",
        "note": {
          "fr": "Trois endroits à lire : `status: NOERROR` (la question a abouti ; `NXDOMAIN` signifierait que le nom n'existe pas), la section `ANSWER` (nom, TTL en secondes, type, valeur) et le drapeau `ad` (*authenticated data*) : le résolveur a vérifié la signature DNSSEC. Les adresses que vous obtiendrez peuvent être différentes, elles changent avec le temps.",
          "en": "Three places to read: `status: NOERROR` (the question succeeded; `NXDOMAIN` would mean the name does not exist), the `ANSWER` section (name, TTL in seconds, type, value) and the `ad` flag (*authenticated data*): the resolver verified the DNSSEC signature. The addresses you get may differ, they change over time."
        }
